{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-970fcaad9e0902eb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-9013 Decomposition — FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done. Priority: Critical. The strategy makes the\n13\tmodel serving stack (KServe + Platform Operator) run on FIPS-enabled OpenShift\n14\tclusters using validated cryptographic modules, assembles a compliance evidence\n15\tpackage, submits it through the security review board to an external accredited\n16\tcertification laboratory, adds CI regression enforcement, and documents the\n17\tFIPS support posture.\n18\t\n19\tTriage (Step 0): neither check fired. The strategy spans two components\n20\t(KServe, Platform Operator), multiple work streams (image remediation, CI\n21\tenforcement, evidence/certification process, documentation), and HLRs at three\n22\tpriority levels. Proceeded to full decomposition.\n23\t\n24\t## Epic List\n25\t\n26\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n27\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS crypto module inventory and compliance gap analysis for serving-stack images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | KServe | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | Remediate KServe images for FIPS 140-3 validated cryptography | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | KServe | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | Remediate Platform Operator image for FIPS 140-3 validated cryptography | Implementation | RHOAI Operator | Platform Operator | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | CI gating check for FIPS crypto regressions on serving-stack builds | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | KServe | P1 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | Assemble FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | KServe | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 | Security review board submission and external lab certification | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | KServe | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E007 | Document FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | Documentation | P0 |\n35\t\n36\tNotes on the epic list:\n37\t\n38\t- E007's priority is P0 by the docs-authoring priority exception: the strategy\n39\t  frontmatter priority is Critical, which maps to P0. This intentionally\n40\t  overrides the P2 of HLR-4 (see Health Warnings).\n41\t- All components are canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`\n42\t  (KServe, Platform Operator, Documentation). No non-obvious mappings were\n43\t  needed. The strategy phrase \"serving data plane\" is interpreted as the\n44\t  serving-stack images in declared scope (see Health Warnings).\n45\t\n46\t## Dependency DAG\n47\t\n48\t```mermaid\n49\tgraph TD\n50\t    E001 --> E002\n51\t    E001 --> E003\n52\t    E001 --> E004\n53\t    E001 --> E005\n54\t    E002 --> E005\n55\t    E003 --> E005\n56\t    E005 --> E006\n57\t    E001 --> E007\n58\t    E002 --> E007\n59\t    E003 --> E007\n60\t    E004 --> E007\n61\t    E005 --> E007\n62\t    E006 --> E007\n63\t```\n64\t\n65\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 → E006 → E007 (length 5).\n66\t\n67\t## DAG Justification\n68\t\n69\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n70\t|------|------|-----------|\n71\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The module inventory identifies which crypto libraries in which KServe images are non-validated and in the request path; remediation builds directly against that artifact. |\n72\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | Same inventory defines the Platform Operator image remediation scope. |\n73\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The CI gating check's rules (what counts as non-validated crypto usage) derive from the inventory's validated/non-validated classification. |\n74\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The module inventory is an explicit component of the evidence package (HLR-2). |\n75\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | Build attestations and FIPS-enabled-cluster test results for KServe images are produced by the remediation and consumed by the evidence package. |\n76\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | Same for the Platform Operator image. |\n77\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 12/13 (serial producer → consumer) | The security review board submission and lab certification consume the assembled evidence package; submission cannot start without it. |\n78\t| E001…E006 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built: the docs epic is blocked by all Implementation epics in the strategy, including the certification-submission epic. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n79\t| E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 (different repos, independent) | KServe midstream repos and the Platform Operator repo are separate; remediations proceed in parallel once the inventory exists. |\n80\t| E004 ∥ E002/E003 | Rule 15 (independent work products) | The CI check is defined by the inventory, not by the remediation code; it can be built and validated in parallel with remediation. |\n81\t| E004 ∥ E005 | Rule 14/15 (independent) | CI enforcement and evidence assembly are independent work products; neither builds against the other. |\n82\t\n83\tNo Investigation epics were created: the strategy's Open Questions section\n84\tstates \"None on scope\". The externally controlled lab scheduling and\n85\treview-board cadence are noted as schedule constraints, not unknowns — they do\n86\tnot change which epics exist or what they do, so they are modeled as scope/ACs\n87\ton the submission epic (E006) rather than as Investigations. The module\n88\tinventory (E001) is a required deliverable of HLR-2 and a build-against\n89\tartifact for remediation, so it is an Implementation, not an Investigation.\n90\t\n91\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n92\t\n93\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epics |\n94\t|-----|----------|------------------------|-------|\n95\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Serving-stack images run on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto, none in the request path | E001 (identifies gaps), E002, E003 (remediate + validate) |\n96\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Compliance evidence package assembled and submitted through security review board to external lab | E001 (module inventory), E005 (assembly), E006 (submission/certification) |\n97\t| HLR-3 | P1 | CI gating check fails builds reintroducing non-validated crypto | E004 |\n98\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | E007 |\n99\t\n100\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. Priority inheritance:\n101\tE001 is P0 (transitively enables HLR-1 and HLR-2, both P0). E004 is P1 (enables\n102\tonly HLR-3; nothing downstream depends on it). E002/E003/E005/E006 are P0\n103\t(direct HLR-1/HLR-2 mapping). E007 is P0 via the docs-authoring priority\n104\texception (strategy priority Critical → P0), not via inheritance.\n105\t\n106\t## Health Warnings\n107\t\n108\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n109\t   Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria,\n110\t   and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Team assignments were derived\n111\t   from the architecture context (KServe → RHOAI Serving) and assumed for the\n112\t   Platform Operator (RHOAI Operator) and documentation work (RHOAI Serving).\n113\t   Acceptance criteria below were derived from the HLRs and problem statement.\n114\t2. **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file.** Only\n115\t   `architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` was provisioned.\n116\t   Platform Operator is a canonical component in `.context/rhai-components.txt`\n117\t   and an existing platform component with existing midstream repos, so it was\n118\t   treated as an in-platform active component — no repo-onboarding chain was\n119\t   generated. An onboarding chain would be factually wrong for an existing\n120\t   operator; flagging for human verification of the team assignment.\n121\t3. **Scope ambiguity — \"serving data plane\".** The problem statement says\n122\t   KServe components *and the serving data plane* link non-validated crypto,\n123\t   but the Affected Components table lists only KServe and Platform Operator.\n124\t   Per architecture context, the serving data plane includes Serving Runtimes\n125\t   images (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit), which are NOT in the declared affected\n126\t   components. Decomposition scoped to the declared components; the module\n127\t   inventory (E001) must enumerate the full serving-stack image set, and if it\n128\t   finds non-validated crypto in runtime images outside the declared scope,\n129\t   that needs a follow-up scope decision — it is out of this decomposition.\n130\t4. **Docs priority vs HLR priority.** HLR-4 is P2, but the docs epic is P0 per\n131\t   the mandatory docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter\n132\t   priority Critical → P0). Recorded as an apparent priority inversion\n133\t   explained by the rule; humans may wish to re-plan.\n134\t5. **Human/third-party gates dominate schedule.** Security review board\n135\t   sign-off at defined milestones and the external accredited lab's historical\n136\t   6-9 month turnaround cannot be compressed by engineering effort. These are\n137\t   concentrated in E006, which is scored accordingly\n138\t   (`human_process_gates=-1`, `external_dependency=-1`). The engineering epics\n139\t   (E001-E005) carry no human gates.\n140\t\n141\t## Tiered Delivery\n142\t\n143\tThe external certification laboratory is an external dependency with\n144\tuncertain timing (historical 6-9 month turnaround), and the strategy CAN\n145\tdeliver partial value without it — so a Tier 1 / Tier 2 split applies:\n146\t\n147\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: E001 (inventory), E002/E003 (FIPS-compliant\n148\t  images — HLR-1 delivered), E004 (CI regression enforcement — HLR-3\n149\t  delivered), E005 (assembled evidence package). All of this completes without\n150\t  the lab.\n151\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated)**: E006 — security review board submission and\n152\t  external lab certification (the submission half of HLR-2). Gated by E005 and\n153\t  by the review board / lab cadence.\n154\t\n155\tE007 (docs) spans the tiers: its content (FIPS posture, prerequisites) is\n156\tavailable once Tier 1 lands, but per Rule 11 it is blocked by all\n157\tImplementation epics including E006, so it formally completes last. If\n158\ttime-to-market for documentation matters, humans may wish to split a\n159\tposture-only doc delivery out of Tier 1.\n160\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9719ed011d20632","agent_id":"a51e6c56cfa0c7e4c","session_id":"d93786ac-0085-40fb-b807-b5feb6113de8","uuid":"3de9f400-2f83-4a06-83ac-d2071ed0e243","timestamp":"2026-10-08T15:06:37.477Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-970fcaad9e0902eb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
