{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-840f1e47f58c3996","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9013\n10\t\n11\t## Review Summary\n12\t\n13\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n14\tRecommendation: accept\n15\t\n16\t## Criterion Details\n17\t\n18\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n19\t\n20\tAll four HLRs are mapped and traceable:\n21\t\n22\t- HLR-1 (P0): E001 (gap identification), E002 (KServe remediation), E003 (Platform Operator remediation) — full coverage of the \"run on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto, none in the request path\" requirement.\n23\t- HLR-2 (P0): E001 (module inventory), E005 (assembly), E006 (submission through review board to lab) — all three named evidence components (module inventory, build attestations, test results) are covered.\n24\t- HLR-3 (P1): E004 (CI gating check).\n25\t- HLR-4 (P2): E007 (documentation).\n26\t\n27\tPriority inheritance is correct: E001 is P0 (transitively enables the P0 HLR-1/HLR-2 work and blocks all of it); E004 is P1 (enables only HLR-3; nothing except the docs epic depends on it, and Rule 11 edges do not trigger inheritance); E002/E003/E005/E006 are P0 by direct HLR mapping. E007 is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority Critical → P0), correctly applied and explicitly documented in the epic list notes and Health Warnings. No priority collapse: no epic bundles deferrable lower-priority features with P0 work — E004 (HLR-3) and E007 (HLR-4) are correctly separated into independently plannable epics.\n28\t\n29\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n30\t\n31\tThe DAG is acyclic and every edge is justified:\n32\t\n33\t- Cross-checked the Mermaid diagram against every epic's frontmatter `dependencies` — a perfect match in both directions, with arrows drawn dependency → dependent per convention.\n34\t- All edges reference existing main-plan epics; no branch files exist and no main-plan epic references a branch node.\n35\t- No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so the implementation-type parallelism mandates (rules 7, 8, 10) do not apply.\n36\t- E001 → E002/E003/E004/E005 edges are Rule 13 artifact-consumption edges (the module inventory is a genuine build-against artifact: remediation scope, CI rule set, and evidence content all derive from it) — not configuration references.\n37\t- E002 ∥ E003 (separate repos) and E004 ∥ E002/E003/E005 (independent work products) are correctly left parallel; no naturally parallel work is serialized.\n38\t- E005 → E006 is a correct serial producer → consumer edge (submission cannot start without the assembled package).\n39\t- E001..E006 → E007 are Rule 11 docs-authoring edges — valid, and correctly noted as not triggering priority inheritance.\n40\t- Critical path length 5 (E001 → E002 → E005 → E006 → E007) is within the L-strategy heuristic (5-7) for a two-component strategy spanning remediation plus an externally gated certification process.\n41\t- Content scan for un-captured data dependencies: E004's \"no false failures on remediated images\" concern is correctly handled as an acceptance criterion rather than a spurious edge on E002/E003; no other content-level dependency is missing from the DAG.\n42\t\n43\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n44\t\n45\t- E003 is correctly split from E002 by component and team (Platform Operator / RHOAI Operator vs KServe / RHOAI Serving).\n46\t- E001, E002, E004, E005, E006 share the KServe / RHOAI Serving tuple but are distinct work products (inventory analysis, image remediation, CI enforcement, evidence assembly, external submission management) — correct boundary drawing along the work-product axis.\n47\t- E007 is a separate docs-authoring epic (substantive content authoring), not mixed into any code epic.\n48\t- No scope duplication: no two epics independently