trategy says they are, and the resulting Low classification is well founded.\n66\t- E001-E005 correctly carry `human_process_gates: +1` — engineering work with no approval boards.\n67\t- E007's `human_process_gates: -1` (docs technical review) is a defensible reading of the docs-authoring review AC.\n68\t- E002's `architecture_claims: +1` vs E003's `architecture_claims: 0` correctly reflects that only KServe has an architecture-context file — matching the health warning about Platform Operator.\n69\t- `open_questions: +1` across all epics matches the strategy's \"None on scope\" open-questions section, with the lab/board cadence correctly treated as constraints rather than unknowns.\n70\t\n71\tNo computed-score arithmetic was checked per instructions; signal values and rationales are consistent and justified.\n72\t\n73\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n74\t\n75\t- Every epic has concrete, testable ACs derived from the HLRs and problem statement (e.g., \"no non-validated cryptographic modules in the request path, per the module inventory classification\"; \"a serving-stack build that reintroduces non-validated crypto usage in the request path fails the gating check\"; \"InferenceService CRs reach Ready with correct predictor status\").\n76\t- Rule 24: the docs-authoring epic (E007) has the mandated \"technically reviewed against the delivered implementation\" AC — present and specific.\n77\t- Rule 25 and Rule 23 do not apply (no konflux-onboarding chain; FIPS remediation is not a user-facing capability replacement).\n78\t- No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — the bodies are self-contained (verified by scanning all seven epic bodies; dependencies are described in plain terms like \"the module inventory\" and captured in frontmatter).\n79\t- E006's ACs appropriately capture the externally controlled gates (board sign-off at milestones, lab acceptance, tracked feedback items, visible schedule expectations).\n80\t\n81\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n82\t\n83\t- All strategy scope is covered: image remediation (E002/E003), evidence package assembly and submission (E005/E006), CI regression enforcement (E004), documentation (E007), with the inventory (E001) as the shared foundation. No strategy capability or HLR acceptance criterion is unaccounted for.\n84\t- Out-of-scope boundaries are respected: workbench/pipeline/training FIPS posture and Common Common Criteria are excluded; the \"serving data plane\" ambiguity is explicitly flagged as a health warning and handled correctly — E001 must report findings outside declared scope as out-of-scope findings for a scope decision, not remediate them.\n85\t- Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: the 6-9 month lab turnaround and review-board cadence are explicit in E006's scope, ACs, and signal scoring; the \"cannot be compressed by engineering effort\" constraint is reflected in the Tiered Delivery split.\n86\t- Tiered delivery is correctly evaluated: Tier 1 (E001-E005, delivers HLR-1 and HLR-3 plus the assembled package) vs Tier 2 (E006, externally gated), with the docs epic's tier-spanning behavior explicitly noted.\n87\t- No conditional branches are required — the strategy has no bounded-outcome unknowns.\n88\t- Documentation support: HLR-4 explicitly requires documentation and the epic set includes the docs-authoring epic E007 — Rule 11 satisfied.\n89\t- Missing strategy sections (teams table, dependencies table, GWT ACs, NFRs) are transparently recorded as health warnings rather than silently papered over; team assignments are flagged for human verification where derived.\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fe55be0a2402c38","agent_id":"a91bcf1ecefbf2f61","session_id":"d93786ac-0085-40fb-b807-b5feb6113de8","uuid":"613856a6-7318-4beb-bfa7-78cab0a49c7a","timestamp":"2026-10-08T15:13:02.510Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-840f1e47f58c3996","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent